From 594549d9bf7d6cee87db82fe2a3b6b1bc593f4af Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ReArc Overlay <overlay@rearc.invalid>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 17:14:44 +0400
Subject: [PATCH] P-TAB-001 download event batches: history slices and archived
 pages yield to the host's run loop

The history load's slices, the bridge's drain of their created events and
the host's layout ran as one main-thread stall (17-75 ms at 39,413 rows):
the next slice was an immediate task, so the host's run loop never ran out
of work between slices. DownloadHistory now creates items in slices of
about 4 ms (8 ms before) and posts the next slice 4 ms later, after the
slice's events have been delivered, for the initial load and for a restore;
the run loop then sleeps and commits a frame between slices. A 1 ms delay
came due while the events were delivered and changed nothing.

ReArcDownloadQueryArchived posts each completion 1 ms after the worker's
copy returns: a host that asks for the next page from a page's callback got
every page in one burst. A drain over its 8 ms budget continues in a task
1 ms later instead of at once.

ABI 1.49 download_event_batches: ReArcEngineSetDownloadBatchCallback.
While set, every download event goes to it instead of the per-event
callback, in the same order relative to every other host callback: events
queued back to back (a slice's created events) arrive in one call, at most
1024 per call, each with its item's start and end times
(ReArcDownloadTimes), so the host makes no ReArcDownloadGetTimes call per
restored item. A queued batch is extended only while nothing else has been
posted after it (ABIPostCount). Hosts that do not set it keep one task per
event. No earlier struct changed.

Bridge 0.52.0, ABI 1.49.

Last rebased: 2026-11-07 onto Chromium 156.0.8078.74 (d331de35ca).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
